  <title>2.10.1: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00542</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.10.1: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cc1f5639-a04b-442b-b877-18efc5406adb</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Kiran is getting dinner for his drama club on the evening of their final rehearsal. He plans to buy some prepared dishes from a supermarket.</para>
<list>
  <item>The budget for the dinner is $60.</item>
  <item>The prepared dishes are sold by the pound, at $5.29 per pound. </item>
  <item>He also plans to buy two large bottles of sparkling water at $2.49 each.</item>
</list>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Represent the constraints in the situation mathematically. If you use variables, specify what each one means.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(5.29p+2(2.49)\leq60\), or \(5.29p+4.98\leq60\). \(p\) is the pounds of prepared foods Kiran could buy without going over budget.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How many pounds of prepared dishes can Kiran buy? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    Enter your answer here:
    
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Up to 10.4 pounds, or \(p\leq10.4\). For example: After removing the cost of sparkling water, Kiran still has $55.02. Dividing that amount by 5.29 gives 10.4.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
